<article class="env-definition" id="00WP">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00WP">Definition <span data-tag="00WP">7.12.1</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a category. We say that a family $\{ U_ i \to U\} _{i \in I}$ is an <em>effective epimorphism</em> if all the morphisms $U_ i \to U$ are representable (see Categories, Definition <a href="/tag/001X" data-tag="001X">4.6.4</a>), and for any $X\in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$ the sequence </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _\mathcal {C}(U, X) \ar[r] &  \prod \nolimits _{i \in I} \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _\mathcal {C}(U_ i, X) \ar@<1ex>[r] \ar@<-1ex>[r] &  \prod \nolimits _{(i, j) \in I^2} \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _\mathcal {C}(U_ i \times _ U U_ j, X) }  \]
</div>
  <p> is an equalizer diagram. We say that a family $\{ U_ i \to U\} $ is a <em>universal effective epimorphism</em> if for any morphism $V \to U$ the base change $\{ U_ i \times _ U V \to V\} $ is an effective epimorphism. </p>
</article>